        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-10-26T14:12:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Can it be something else than void*? What stops us from using a proper name here\n\nI guess the idea is that it can be abstract. In this particular implementation of pkvm_mm_ops (used for guest page tables) this pointer is used as a memcache pointer, in other implementations it may be used for anything else. (Currently in those other implementations, i.e. for host and hypervisor page tables, it is not used at all.)",
      "parentUuid": "1bb6f8e2_f1a69d0f",
      "revId": "d8cd595401ec40e98bfb84c648b7fa45d7ab59be",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e2cd1558_b5c5970d",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/hyp/pgtable.h",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 17,
      "author": {
        "id": 1076784
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-10-26T18:11:10Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Not sure how that would actually work. If you do not have neither common API (argument is void* to pass different types) nor the common implementation (there are different implementations of zalloc_page() for different cases) then callers need to know which one to call, so abstracting this into \"ops\" structure does not buy us much... I think this only works if it is purely a pass-through mechanism.",
      "parentUuid": "49aad87b_24dd8c59",
      "revId": "d8cd595401ec40e98bfb84c648b7fa45d7ab59be",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "764d54f0_0666f6cc",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/hyp/pgtable.h",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 17,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-10-27T10:15:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It is basically pass-through. The users of the pgtable API _implement_ these ops, including zalloc_page(), and those same users call into the pgtable API which passes the memcache pointer down to zalloc_page(). There is no logic in pgtable.c that would depend on it being specifically a memcache, not just an arbitrary pointer.\n\nOTOH, the fact that pkvm_pgtable_map() is the only function among the pgtable API functions that allows the users to pass this pointer (while other functions, which also need to call zalloc_page() in some cases, just pass NULL to it) suggests a particular assumption about the use case for this pointer. Plus, the internal `struct pkvm_pgtable_map_data`\u0027s field is named `memcache`, not `arg` or something...\n\nSo as far as I\u0027m concerned, I\u0027m ok with either leaving it as `void *` or changing it to `struct pkvm_memcache *`. We can always change it either way in the future if needed.",
      "parentUuid": "e2cd1558_b5c5970d",
      "revId": "d8cd595401ec40e98bfb84c648b7fa45d7ab59be",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "830aa653_58b6a784",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/vmx/pkvm/hyp/pgtable.h",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 17,
      "author": {
        "id": 3484945
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-10-27T10:33:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "To be honest I do not have strong opinion here either: probably I will switch to `struct pkvm_memcache *` agreeing that we can change it back to `void *` in the future if needed.",
      "parentUuid": "764d54f0_0666f6cc",
